					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=348648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608196113" alt="sirpudding" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=348648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;" >sirpudding</a></span> <span class="odate time_1542037442 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Nov 2018 15:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4056796">
									

<p>Not rules <em>changes</em> necessarily, there may not be any, as you say, but new rule content. In general gaming audiences aren't looking for pure narrative, they've got a plethora of fiction to be inspired by already. Good gaming writing usually has enough lore and flavor text to transform the rules statistics into more than just a long list of numbers.</p>
<p>So for a player-oriented article like this, I would include things like the setting's unique races available for PCs, some setting specific gear they can buy, new spells, (for a class based system like D&amp;D) some setting-specific class options, and if this is 5th edition some new Backgrounds that really make a PC part <em>this</em> specific game world.</p>
<p>Since this is a religion focused article, I would cut the material that's not related to that, and replace it with settling specific options for clerics and paladins, like archetypes, backgrounds, and spells for those classes.</p>

							</div>

								
