					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/monteparnas" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2926286); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2926286&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608196113" alt="Monteparnas" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2926286)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/monteparnas" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2926286); return false;" >Monteparnas</a></span> <span class="odate time_1543621855 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Nov 2018 23:50</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4072600">
									

<p>As a setting first description, it is ok for me. Not particularly engaging, and already the third, as stated before, but I kinda dig it.</p>
<p>But as a tale, it is weak. Even as a D&amp;D+Fallout+SCP tale (and I like all three). Mostly because it is in a form of explanation that I hardly see anyone talking to anyone else.</p>
<p>In the end, it is too obviously a setting description to the readers, not to other in-setting characters. So I suggest that you take it too its fullest. Stopping asking if people want you to write more, and just do it. This won't work as a tale, but it will work as a canon's hub. And then you can explore this setting in detail. I do see some promise here, but you must go further for it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
